{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1178 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "69a5e3c84894beb1e9650feae540348b2a4b1793faee97b4798fa6f8f4305b2f" :edb/db-before-t 1177 :edb/db-after-t 1178 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534490 50 #inst "2026-10-11T22:37:56.781Z" 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423031 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186087852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423030 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186092274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423032 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186092328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423029 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186096666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423033 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186153892 1078 #edb/ref 17592186153896 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186157608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186157608 1071 "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}x^{4}\\tan xdx = A$, what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}x^{4}\\tan xdx$?" 13194139534490 17592186045417 false] [17592186157608 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186157608 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ A+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186374485 1071 "![](images/d1/d13bfa3f5b0a9456c3251e9363c132578ba0d93b1b5a76c5b375c0c1abc7e310.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$\\}$" 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186374485 1071 "![](images/d1/d13bfa3f5b0a9456c3251e9363c132578ba0d93b1b5a76c5b375c0c1abc7e310.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$}$" 13194139534490 17592186045417 false] [17592186374485 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(5,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(5)^{2}+16(0)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64\\}.\n$$" 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186374485 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(5,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(5)^{2}+16(0)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$" 13194139534490 17592186045417 false] [17592186374486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423008 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186374486 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374487 13194139534490 17592186045417 false] [17592186374486 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423008 13194139534490 17592186045417 true] [17592186422997 1043 #edb/ref 17592186423034 13194139534490 17592186045417 true